  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Class-A malls and Premium Outlets keep taking share from weaker retail real estate: occupancy holds ~96%, rent spreads stay positive, recaptured Saks/Macy's boxes re-lease at higher rents and the $2B+ mixed-use pipeline delivers. AFFO/unit compounds ~3-4% after refinancing drag, the dividend keeps rising, and Eli Simon's planned succession proves uneventful. Capitalised at a 9.75% cost of equity this is worth less than the current price: the market is pricing a lower discount rate than the house uses.",
    "bull": "Leasing momentum (new deals +20% y/y, retailer sales/sf +13.9%) persists, redevelopment yields 7-8% on cost, international outlets (Italy, Japan, Korea) add growth, and lower long rates compress the cost of equity to 9%. Real Estate FFO/unit grows 5-6% near term with margins held as NOI growth offsets refinancing.",
    "bear": "A consumer recession plus tariff pressure on apparel tenants triggers a new wave of retailer and department-store bankruptcies; occupancy slips, percentage rent falls, and maturities refinance at 5.5-6%, compressing post-interest FFO margins by ~400bp. Leadership transition after David Simon's death loses deal-making edge. AFFO flat-to-down for three years, cost of equity 10.75%."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "SPG is valued as a REIT: the stream an owner of a common share or OP unit actually receives the economic benefit of is AFFO -- Real Estate FFO less straight-line rent, recurring re-leasing capex, redevelopment spend and SBC -- and because it is already after interest it is capitalised at the cost of equity with no net-debt deduction. GAAP net income (revaluation and remeasurement gains) and consolidated FCF (before JV share and NCI) are not used.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Cost of equity is the dominant lever: +/-50bp moves base IV roughly -/+8-9%; the 10y UST print (~5.24%) drives it.",
      "Refinancing drag: $35.7B of our-share debt rolling to ~5.4% from a lower book rate compresses post-interest FFO margins; the base case assumes ~150bp over five years.",
      "Anchor/department-store attrition (Macy's closures through 2028, Saks/Exemplar) and tenant bankruptcies in a consumer downturn.",
      "Redevelopment execution and capital intensity: the capex path assumes ~$0.5B/yr SPG-share development through FY29; overruns or slow lease-up reduce AFFO conversion.",
      "Other platform investments (retailer stakes) carried at zero -- an upside optionality not in the value, but their losses are also excluded from the stream.",
      "Leadership transition after the death of David Simon (March 2026)."
    ]
  },
